**Zeros**

Properties of the zeros of \(I_{r}(z)\) and \(K_{s}(z)\) may be deduced from those of \(J_{r}(z)\) and \(H_{\nu}^{(1)}(z)\) respectively, by application of the transformations **9.6.3** and **9.6.4**.

For example, if \(r\) is real the zeros of \(I_{r}(z)\) are all complex unless \(-2k{<}_{\nu}{<}{-}(2k{-}1)\) for some positive integer \(k\), in which event \(I_{r}(z)\) has two real zeros.

The approximate distribution of the zeros of \(K_{n}(z)\) in the region \(-{3\over 2}\pi{\leq}{\rm arg}~{}z{\leq}{1\over 2}\pi\) is obtained on rotating **Figure 9.6** through an angle \(-{1\over 2}\pi\) so that the cut lies along the positive imaginary axis. The zeros in the region \(-{3\over 2}\pi{\leq}{\rm arg}~{}z{\leq}{3\pi}\) are their conjugates. \(K_{n}(z)\) has no zeros in the region \(|{\rm arg}~{}z|{\leq}{1\over 2}\pi\); this result remains true when \(n\) is replaced by any real number \(r\).

